<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolesław III the Generous</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1291">1291</a>–<a href="/years/1352">1352</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolesław III the Generous was a Silesian Piast duke, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-the-fat">Henry V of Legnica</a>. In 1311 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a> on the Oder as his share of the inheritance, creating the Duchy of Brzeg, and he also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> for a time. In 1329 he did homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, bringing his duchy under the Bohemian crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-the-generous.jpg" alt="Bolesław III the Generous" width="320" height="732" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albrecht Bräuer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boleslaus_von_Liegnitz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bolesław was born on 23 September 1291, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-the-fat">Henry V the Fat</a> and Elisabeth, daughter of Bolesław the Pious of Greater Poland. He was five when his father died in 1296. His mother and his uncle Bolko I acted as regents until their deaths, and after a brief guardianship by Bishop Henry of Würben, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a> took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1302 and betrothed him to his youngest daughter Margareta. The king's favour made Bolesław a rival to the king's own relatives, and after the murder of Wenceslaus III in 1306 he styled himself heir of the Polish Kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His forces were too weak for the contest: he failed to hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a> in 1306 and 1307, gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> in 1308 and gave up his claim at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> on 11 June 1311 for 8,000 pieces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. His ambitions exhausted his money, and in 1311 he had to divide the inheritance with his brothers Henry VI and Władysław. Given first choice, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a>, the smallest share, for the compensation it brought, and a year later took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> from Władysław, who could not pay his part.</p><p>Allied with Władysław I the Elbow-high, he fought the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a> in 1312 to 1317, gaining Wołów and Lubiąż, and a second war ended in the peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> on 10 August 1323. King John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> made him governor of Bohemia in 1321 and 1322. He quarrelled with Henry VI over Wrocław and attacked it in 1324 and again while King John was away from Bohemia, failing both times. When Władysław returned in 1329 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> in King John's name, Bolesław, lacking the means to fight, declared himself John's vassal at Wrocław on 9 May 1329.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His lavish life and travels, among them the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a> of 1335, forced him to raise taxes and sell lands. He was excommunicated twice, in 1337 for late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> and in 1340 for seizing Church property, yet endowed Lubiąż Abbey and founded Franciscan and Dominican houses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a>. In 1342 he gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> to his sons Wenceslaus I and Louis I and retired to Brzeg with his second wife, Katharina Šubić of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, where he died on 21 April 1352. He was buried at Lubiąż, and Brzeg passed to his widow.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bolesław III the Generous” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1311 the sons of Duke Henry V of Legnica divided their inheritance, and <b>Bolesław III the Generous</b> took Brzeg (Brieg) on the Oder as his duchy, separating it from the Duchy of Legnica.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-brzeg">Duchy of Brzeg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.87,17.47&amp;year=1330" aria-label="Brzeg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4947 399 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Bolesław III the Generous"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5397 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5397 669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.87,17.47&amp;year=1330">What was here in 1330? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
